dep `data.map:a`.\n- Set: `data.has('a')` -> dep `data.set:a`.\n- Array: `list[0]` -> dep `list.0`; `list.includes('x')` -> maybe `list.0...`.\n- Multi-level selector chains propagation.\n- Atomic updates.\n- Circular safety.\n- Health API.\n- React integration.\n\nWe need to be careful: Kea's selectors typically read reducer selectors which return **immutable state values**. The proxies are transient\u2014they exist only while a selector is being evaluated. We return proxies to downstream selectors during evaluation. But after evaluation, the cached result stored should be the **real value**, not the proxy (to avoid leaking proxies and to ensure Object.is comparisons work). Actually if we store the proxy as cached result, then React's getSnapshot would return a proxy; but the proxy forwards all operations to the real object, so it works. But `Object.is` between two different proxies of the same underlying value would be false, causing spurious re-renders. So we must store the real value as cached, and only use proxies transiently while evaluating dependent selectors.\n\nSo flow:\n- `atomicSelector` wrapper for name N:\n  - If not dirty, return `cachedResult[N]` (real value).\n  - If dirty (or first run):\n    - Begin capture session (tracker = new DependencyTracker with base path = N? Actually the wrapper's base path for returned value is N).\n    - Compute args: each arg is either a selector wrapper (which when called runs with its own tracking; but from the perspective of the caller, the caller records a dependency on the callee's **leaf path within callee output** only when it accesses properties of the returned value). So the caller should NOT record `selector:N` as a leaf dep directly; instead it records via property access on the returned proxy. But if callee returns a primitive (e.g., `bookId`), the caller doesn't access any property; then we need to record the dependency as `bookId`. So when the caller invokes callee wrapper and gets a primitive, the caller