af path `A`; B's dep `A` matches).\n3. If B is then evaluated, it will recompute.\n\nBut note: step 1 already marks B dirty for `userInfo.name`? No, because state change is `name`, and `userInfo.name` doesn't match `name`. So B relies on step 2. But step 2 only runs when `userInfo` is evaluated. If `userInfo` is not dirty? It should be dirty because it depends on `name`. But will it be evaluated? It's evaluated when someone calls it. When B is evaluated, B calls A (userInfo) as an input; A evaluates (dirty), then A propagates to B. But B was already being evaluated... Let's think about ordering.\n\nThe evaluation of B:\n- `atomicSelector('B', state, props)`:\n  - Check if B dirty. It might not be marked dirty yet if propagation hasn't happened (e.g., React calls B directly after dispatch; B's dirty flag was not set because propagation through A hasn't run).\n  - If not dirty, it would return cached result, missing the update. So we can't rely solely on dirty flags propagated lazily. We need B to check whether its dependencies have changed **at evaluation time**.\n\nBetter approach: B doesn't rely on a precomputed dirty flag; instead, at evaluation time, B checks each dependency:\n- For each direct selector dependency (the callee selectors it calls as inputs), it evaluates them (ensuring they're up to date) and then checks whether the **leaf portions it reads** changed. How? The callee wrappers, when called during B's evaluation, can indicate if their output changed (or if the relevant leaves changed). If A's output changed in a way that affects B's deps, B re-evaluates.\n- For state leaf deps (which are tracked as leaf paths under root reducer selectors), the state diff at dispatch time can mark them dirty. But B's deps are leaf paths like `user.name`; when B is called, it can also just compare the actual current leaf value against the value it used last time? That's complex.\n\nSimplest robust architecture: **Eager invalidation on every action**, including propagation through the dependency graph, but done by evaluating all affected selectors? That would violate \"propagation only to affected selectors\" and could be expensive. But the requirement says \"If a selector's inputs haven't changed, it should not re-evaluate.\" Eagerly evaluating all would violate that if we evaluate unaffected selectors. However we can propagate lazily by maintaining, for each selector, a \"dependency versions\" mechanism.\n\nLet me think of a version-based invalidation:\n- Each leaf path in the state has a version (global counter incremented on each action if that leaf changed). Actually we can store a `version` for each path.\n- Each selector records, for each dependency, the version of that dependency at the time it was computed.\n- On evaluation, for each dep, look up current version and compare to recorded version. If any differs, selector is dirty.\n- For selector deps (e.g., `capitalizedName`), the \"version\" of that dependency is the version of the underlying state leaves that feed it? But if `capitalizedName` is derived from `name`, then its \"version\" should change when `name` changes. We could assign each selector a `computedVersion` that increments each time its output recomputes (or more precisely, when its relevant output changes). Then B stores `capitalizedNameVersion`; if changed, B recomputes.\n\nBut if B depends on `user.name` (leaf within A's output), and A recomputes due to `user.age` changing (but `.name` same), B should NOT recompute. With per-selector computedVersion, B would see A's version increment and recompute \u2014 wrong. So we need per-leaf versions within selector outputs.\n\nAlternative: store in each dependency the **value** of the leaf at last compute, and compare on evaluation. For state leaves: when B computes, it reads actual state values (via the underlying real state) for its deps and compares with stored. For selector leaf deps: when B computes, it can ask A for the leaf value. But comparing arbitrary values each time may be expensive but correct. Actually simpler: for each dep path, we can store a \"version\" that is incremented whenever that specific path changes. For state paths, increment version on dispatch diff. For selector-output paths, when A recomputes, we diff A's old/new output and increment versions for the changed leaf paths (relative to A's name). Then B compares dep versions.\n\nLet's define a global per-context (or per-logic) `versions: Map<string, number>` where keys are full relative paths within a logic slice, e.g. `user.name`, `user`, `capitalizedName`. Actually we can use one map per logic to avoid collisions. On each dispatch:\n1. Diff state slice old vs new -> set of changed leaf paths `P` (relative to logic root).\n2. For each path p in P, increment `versions[p]`.\n3. Also mark all selectors dirty if any dep matches p (dep == p or dep prefix of p). But maybe we don't even need dirty flags; version comparison at eval time suffices. But for dirtyCause and evaluation counting, we can compute at eval time.\n\nWhen selector A recomputes:\n1. Diff old output vs new output relative to A's name -> changed leaf paths `Q` (e.g., `A.name`, `A.age`).\n2. For each q in Q, increment `versions[q]`.\n3. This will cause dependents to detect version changes when they evaluate.\n\nAt selector evaluation time:\n- For each dep `d`, get current version `versions.get(d)` (or 0 if undefined) and compare to `lastVersions.get(d)` stored. If any differ, selector is dirty.\n- But wait: for dep `user.name`, version increments on state change `user.name`; so B detects dirty when evaluated. For dep `userInfo.name` where userInfo is derived, version increments when A recomputes (via diff). So B detects. Great \u2014 no need for dirty flags/pre-propagation; evaluation-time version check works.\n- However, there's a problem: if B's dep is `capitalizedName` (whole derived selector output), and `capitalizedName` recomputes but produces the **same string** (e.g., name changes case but same normalized value), the diff old vs new output would produce no changed leaves (since values equal), so version doesn't increment and B won't recompute. That's correct: B's input didn't change.\n- If A recomputes but output object is new with same leaf values, diff yields no changed leaves -> no propagation. Correct.\n\nThis version-based lazy approach seems elegant. But we need to ensure `lastVersions` are stored per selector and that B records its deps correctly each time (since deps may change). Also for **state** dep paths, we need versions incremented on state diff. For a dep like `user` (whole object), version increments on `user.name` change? We said a change at `user.name` should invalidate a dep `user` (since d is prefix of p). So at eval time, for dep `d`, we should check not just `versions.get(d)` but whether any changed path under `d` occurred. We can store a global `changeLog` (list of changed paths per dispatch or per recompute) and at eval time check if any change path matches dep. But versions per exact path don't capture \"descendant changed\". E.g., dep `user`, changes `user.name` -> we need to know. We could increment a \"descendant version\" for every ancestor path too. When a leaf path changes, increment versions for that path AND all its ancestors (prefixes). So `user.name` change increments `user.name`, `user`, and maybe root. Then dep `user` detects. Dep `user.name` detects. Dep `user.name.length` (not a real path) doesn't. Good. That's simple: when diffing changed leaves, for each changed leaf path, increment version at that path and all ancestor paths. But doing this for every dispatch with deep paths is fine.\n\nSimilarly when A recomputes and changed leaf `A.name` occurs, increment versions for `A.name` and ancestors `A`. So dep `A` (whole selector) detects when any leaf in A's output changes. Dep `A.name` detects when that leaf changes. \n\nNow, there's a subtlety with maps/sets: changed path `data.map:a` and its ancestor `data`. We'll treat `map:a` as a leaf component; the ancestor path for `data.map:a` is `data`. For prefix matching, `data` is prefix. Good.\n\nNow, how do we compute changed leaf paths from state diff? Let's write a `diffPaths(oldVal, newVal, base)`:\n- If oldVal === newVal, return [].\n- If both are Map: compare sizes and each key. For each key, if old.has(k) !== new.has(k), record `${base}.map:${k}`. If both have k and old.get(k) !== new.get(k), record `${base}.map:${k}` (and recurse? Map values are leaves; if map value is object, should we recurse? The dependency for map access is at the key level \u2014 \"Map key access, `<reducer>.map:<key>`\". So even if the value object changes internally, the dep is still `map:key`. We can just record `map:key` without recursing into value. That's fine-grained enough at the map level.)\n- If both are Set: compare membership; for each value present in one but not both, record `${base}.set:${value}`. For values in both, if the value object changes? Set membership is identity-based, so values are either present or not; no recursion.\n- If both are Array: compare length; if length differs, record indices beyond min length? For each index i in 0..max-1: if old[i] !== new[i], record `${base}.${i}` and recurse into element if both objects. Also if length changed, we could record changed indices plus maybe the whole array? The deps are indices read; if array grows, element at new index is \"changed\". We'll record indices where values differ.\n- If both are plain objects: for union of keys, if old[k] !== new[k], record `${base}.${k}` and recurse into value if both are objects/arrays/maps/sets (to get deeper leaves). Actually for granularity we need to recurse: if `user` object changes, record `user.name` not just `user`. So recurse for object/array values. But for primitive values, record the leaf.\n- Otherwise (different primitives or types): record `${base}`.\n\nOne concern: if a nested object is replaced with a completely different object and some keys removed, recursion still records leaf changes for keys present in both or added. For removed keys, record `${base}.${k}`. Good.\n\nNow, what about state diff for the **whole store** vs logic slice? We need changed paths relative to each logic's root. We could diff the whole store once and then for each logic map to local. But simpler: each logic's engine, on dispatch, diffs `logic.selector(oldState)` vs `logic.selector(newState)` (the logic slice). That's O(slice size) per logic, which could be expensive with many logics. But acceptable for tests. To optimize, we can diff whole store once and distribute paths. But implementing mapping is a bit more code. Let's consider: each logic's root is at `logic.path` (e.g. `['scenes','something']`). The global state diff could produce global paths like `scenes.something.user.name`. For each logic, strip the prefix. But also there could be multiple logic at different paths. Let's implement a global diff in the context and then have each logic engine subscribe to the store and compute its own slice diff. Simpler to keep per-logic diff for correctness. But the state can be huge and this runs on every dispatch for every mounted logic. For this task, acceptable.\n\nActually, we can hook into Redux via middleware or store.subscribe. The store's subscribe callback receives no args; we need previous state. We can create a middleware that captures `getState()` before/after and calls `engine.notify(prevState, nextState)`. Or we can modify the store subscription. Since `useSelector` uses `store.subscribe`, and the engine also needs to update versions before React selectors run, the order matters: engine must process the new state before React's getSnapshot calls. Middleware runs during dispatch, before subscribers. So a middleware is ideal: after `next(action)`, get new state, call engine notify. That way by the time subscribers run, versions are updated.\n\nBut there may be multiple logics. We can have a per-context registry of atomic engines, and a middleware installed when `atomicSelectors` is enabled. Or simpler: each engine can subscribe to the store and handle batching. But middleware is cleaner.\n\nWait \u2014 there's also the `batchChanges` mechanism that dispatches `@KEA/FLUSH` after a batch. With atomic selectors, the engine should probably process after the actual state-changing action, and React's subscription is paused during mount. But engine processing during batch could still work. The key: versions updated before React getSnapshot. Middleware does that.\n\nHowever, the task says \"The new engine intercepts core lifecycle hooks; valid implementations must ensure that standard plugin event ordering (e.g., afterMount) is not disrupted.\" This hints they expect the engine implemented as a **plugin** that hooks into `beforeMount`/`afterMount` etc. Let's think about implementing as a plugin. But it could also be built into core selectors. The mention \"intercepts core lifecycle hooks\" suggests we should add an `atomicSelectors` plugin that hooks `afterMount` to set up subscriptions and `beforeUnmount` to tear down. But we also need to modify selector creation. \n\nLet's design as a plugin or integrated? Given the requirement to make `logic.selectorHealth()` available and to track deps, and to preserve lifecycle ordering, we can implement:\n- Add `atomicSelectors` option to context.\n- In `src/core/selectors.ts` and `src/core/reducers.ts`, when context option atomicSelectors is true, use an atomic selector factory instead of reselect.\n- Add engine setup in build/mount lifecycle.\n\nLet me think about where to put the engine. Could create `src/core/atomic-selectors.ts` with the engine. \n\nLet's define the core data structures:\n\n```ts\ninterface AtomicSelectorMeta {\n  name: string\n  compute: (...args:any[]) => any\n  args: Selector[]\n  deps: Set<string>          // relative leaf path dependencies\n  dependents: Set<string>\n  lastDeps: Map<string, number> // dep