root read). \n\nLet's refine the tracker:\n- For each selector evaluation, the tracker has a root tracking state (the logic slice proxy) used by reducer selectors to record direct state deps. When a reducer selector reads `state.user`, it records a direct state dep `user` (leaf path).\n- When a user selector B calls another selector A via the `s.user` proxy:\n  - It records a \"selector dep\" on A, initially whole-output (`A`), refined by property access to `A.prop...`.\n  - In the final deps list, a whole-output selector dep is stored as `A` but marked as selectorDep. When A's output changes (version of `A` increments because a leaf under A changed), dirtyCause is `selector:A`. If refined to `A.prop`, store as `A.prop` and treat as state leaf dep (cause `A.prop`).\n\nBut for a root reducer selector A, when B reads whole A output (e.g., `books` object used wholesale), the dep `books` is also a state leaf. If `books.1` changes, version of `books` increments (ancestor), so B would be invalidated with cause... should it be `selector:books` or `books`? Since B used the whole books object, a change to any book invalidates B; cause could be `books` (raw leaf) or `selector:books`. Spec says `selector:<localName>` when caused by another selector. B was caused by `books` selector output changing. Either is arguably fine. To keep it simple and match \"local selector names\" in dependencies, I'd say for whole-output selector deps, use `selector:<name>` as cause and list `name` in dependencies. For refined leaf deps, use raw path as cause and list path in dependencies.\n\nSo metadata per selector:\n- `dependencies: Set<string>` \u2014 leaf paths and local selector names (as displayed).\n- `dependents: Set<string>`\n- `evaluations`\n- `dirtyCause`\n- `lastDepVersions: Map<string, number>`\n- `depKinds: Map<string, 'selector'|'state'>` \u2014 to determine cause formatting. For dependency `books`, kind selector. For `user.name`, kind state.\n- `compute`, `args`, `cached`, `versions` internal.\n\nWait, but for `user.name`, if `user` is a root reducer selector, B's dep `user.name` is kind state. For a derived selector `userInfo`, B's dep `userInfo.name` is also kind state (raw path cause `userInfo.name`) per spec example? The spec says dependencies are relative paths like `user.name`; dirtyCause raw leaf paths e.g. `user.name` when caused by state change. It doesn't mention derived selector property causes. So `userInfo.name` as raw path is fine.\n\nNow versions:\n- Global per-logic `versions: Map<string, number>`.\n- On state diff: for each changed leaf path p (relative to logic root), increment version for p and all ancestors. But for deps like `user.name`, version at `user.name` increments. For dep `user`, version at `user` increments (ancestor). Good.\n- On selector A recompute: diff old vs new output relative to A name -> changed leaf paths q (e.g., `A.name`, `A.age`). Increment version for q and ancestors. For dep `A` (whole selector), version at `A` increments if any leaf changed. For dep `A.name`, version at `A.name` increments if that leaf changed. Good.\n- At selector B eval: for each dep d in B.deps, check `versions.get(d)` vs B.lastDepVersions.get(d). If differs -> dirty. Determine cause based on depKind. If multiple changed, pick first (insertion order).\n\nNow, to compute changed leaves of A's output relative to A name: we call `diffPaths(oldVal, newVal, A_name)`. If A output is primitive and changed, diff returns `[A_name]`? Our `diffPaths` for non-objects with old!==new returns `[base]`. Good.\n\nNow the tracker proxy. Let's implement a class `DependencyTracker` with:\n- `stateRoot: any` \u2014 the logic slice (real value) wrapped in a proxy for reducer selectors.\n- `versions: Map`, `depsBySelector` etc.\n- For a given evaluation, we need to collect deps. But we need to distinguish deps recorded by the **currently evaluated selector** from deps recorded by callee selectors. When B evaluates and calls A, A's own evaluation will