      "reasoning_content": "Good. Now let's create a reproduction script to understand the expected behavior. But first, let me design more concretely and check how `logic.selectorHealth()` should be exposed.\n\nLet me write a small test script (not a jest test yet) to see how the current code behaves with the atomic engine not present. Actually we need to implement first. Let me carefully plan the code changes.\n\nFiles to modify:\n1. `src/types.ts`:\n   - Add `atomicSelectors: boolean` to `InternalContextOptions`.\n   - Add `selectorHealth` to `BuiltLogicAdditions` or Logic? The built logic should have `selectorHealth?(): ...`. Let's add to `BuiltLogicAdditions` or `Logic` interface. It's only when enabled, so optional.\n   - Define types for health return.\n2. `src/kea/context.ts`: add `atomicSelectors: false` default in options. Also add `atomicEngines` set to Context maybe.\n3. `src/core/atomic-selectors.ts`: new module with engine, tracker, diff, etc.\n4. `src/core/selectors.ts`: use atomic engine when enabled.\n5. `src/core/reducers.ts`: use atomic engine for reducer selectors when enabled.\n6. `src/kea/store.ts`: add middleware when atomicSelectors enabled to notify engines.\n7. `src/kea/build.ts`: attach `selectorHealth` to logic when enabled. Or in core plugin defaults.\n8. `src/kea/context.ts` / types: register engines in context.\n9. `src/index.ts`: maybe export atomic engine helpers? Not necessary.\n\nLet me now design the atomic engine module in detail.\n\n```ts\nimport { BuiltLogic, Selector } from '../types'\nimport { getContext } from '../kea/context'\n\ninterface AtomicSelectorMeta {\n  name: string\n  args: Selector[]\n  func: (...args:any[]) => any\n  dependencies: Set<string>\n  dependents: Set<string>\n  depKinds: Map<string, 'selector'|'state'>\n  lastDepVersions: Map<string, number>\n  cached: any\n  hasCached: boolean\n  evaluations: number\n  dirtyCause: string | null\n  inProgress: boolean  // circular detection\n}\n\nclass AtomicSelectorEngine {\n  logic: BuiltLogic\n  versions = new Map<string, number>()\n  selectors = new Map<string, AtomicSelectorMeta>()\n  sessionStack: Session[] = []\n  pendingNotifications = false\n\n  constructor(logic) { this.logic = logic }\n\n  createReducerSelector(key: string): Selector {\n    const meta = this.createMeta(key, [], (root) => root[key])\n    meta.isReducer = true\n    const wrapper = (state, props) => this.runReducerSelector(key, state, props)\n    return wrapper\n  }\n\n  createSelector(name: string, args: Selector[], func: Function): Selector {\n    this.createMeta(name, args, func)\n    return (state, props) => this.runSelector(name, state, props)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nLet's think about how `runSelector` handles args. We need the selector wrapper to be callable with full state. Let's define:\n\n```ts\nrunSelector(name, state, props) {\n  const meta = this.selectors.get(name)!\n  // circular detection\n  if (meta.inProgress) {\n    throw new Error('[KEA] Circular dependency detected')\n  }\n  const session = new Session(this, meta)\n  this.sessionStack.push(session)\n  meta.inProgress = true\n  try {\n    // compute args\n    const argResults = meta.args.map(arg => {\n      if (typeof arg === 'function' && (arg as any)._atomicSelectorName) {\n        return this.callSelector((arg as any)._atomicSelectorName, state, props)\n      }\n      return arg(state, props)\n    })\n    // check dirty\n    const { dirty, cause } = this.checkDirty(meta)\n    if (!dirty && meta.hasCached) {\n      return meta.cached\n    }\n    // compute\n    const result = meta.func(...argResults)\n    const rawResult = this.unwrap(result)\n    // commit deps\n    this.commitDeps(meta, session)\n    // update versions by diffing old/new\n    this.propagate(meta, meta.hasCached ? meta.cached : undefined, rawResult)\n    meta.cached = rawResult\n    meta.hasCached = true\n    meta.evaluations += 1\n    meta.dirtyCause = cause\n    // update lastDepVersions\n    for (const d of meta.dependencies) {\n      meta.lastDepVersions.set(d, this.versions.get(d) ?? 0)\n    }\n    return rawResult\n  } finally {\n    meta.inProgress = false\n    this.sessionStack.pop()\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBut wait: `argResults` are computed BEFORE checking dirty. If the selector is not dirty, we still computed args (which may run other selectors). Then return cached. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: for arg functions that are not atomic (prop selectors / plain functions), we call `arg(state, props)`. For prop selectors `(_, props) => props.extra`, fine. For connected selectors that are plain functions expecting full state, fine.\n\nNow `callSelector(name, state, props)`:\n```ts\ncallSelector(name, state, props) {\n  const session = this.currentSession // caller session\n  const meta = this.selectors.get(name)\n  if (!meta) {\n    // not an atomic selector of this engine (connected or plain)\n    const value = this.logic.selectors[name](state, props)\n    session?.recordSelectorDep(name, value, false) // whole dep, no proxy? \n    return value\n  }\n  // run callee\n  const result = this.runSelector(name, state, props) // raw\n  // register pending dep in caller session\n  const tracked = session ? session.wrapSelectorResult(name, result) : result\n  return tracked\n}\n```\n\nBut careful: `runSelector` pushes/pops its own session. When called from `callSelector`, `this.currentSession` is the caller's session (because caller's runSelector pushed it and is currently in the middle of `meta.args.map`). So `session` captured before calling runSelector is correct.\n\n`session.wrapSelectorResult(name, value)`:\n- Records a pending selector dep entry `{name, wholeUsed: true, paths: Set()}` if not exists.\n- If value is an object/array/map/set (non-null), return a proxy that records refined paths.\n- If primitive, return value as-is (wholeUsed remains true).\n\nWhen the proxy's get trap is invoked, it adds `name.<prop>` to `entry.paths` and sets `wholeUsed = false`? Actually if multiple props