separately.\n\nLet me design Session:\n\n```ts\nclass Session {\n  engine: AtomicSelectorEngine\n  meta: AtomicSelectorMeta\n  stateDeps = new Set<string>()\n  pendingDeps = new Map<string, { selector: string, whole: boolean, paths: Set<string> }>()\n  dirtyCause: string | null = null\n  rootProxy: any\n  rootRaw: any\n  proxyCache = new Map() // object -> proxy? maybe not needed\n\n  constructor(engine, meta) { ... }\n\n  getRootProxy(state) {\n    if (!this.rootProxy) {\n      this.rootRaw = this.engine.logic.selector(state)\n      this.rootProxy = makeStateProxy(this.rootRaw, '', this)\n    }\n    return this.rootProxy\n  }\n\n  recordStateDep(path) { this.stateDeps.add(path) }\n  recordSelectorDep(name, value) { \n    // called when a selector is called and value is primitive (or if we choose to track whole)\n    if (!this.pendingDeps.has(name)) this.pendingDeps.set(name, {selector:name, whole:true, paths:new Set()})\n    return this.pendingDeps.get(name)!\n  }\n  wrapSelectorResult(name, value) {\n    const entry = this.recordSelectorDep(name, value)\n    if (value !== null && (typeof value === 'object')) {\n      return makeSelectorProxy(value, name, this, entry)\n    }\n    return value\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBut we need to track both direct state deps and selector deps. In `commitDeps`, we combine:\n- For each stateDep path: add to meta.dependencies (kind state).\n- For each pendingDep entry:\n  - If `entry.paths.size === 0`: add `entry.selector` as dep (kind selector).\n  - Else: for each path in entry.paths: add path as dep (kind state).\n\nNow, root proxy recording: when reducer selector `user` runs, it calls `logic.selector(state)` (which returns raw root), then accesses `.user` on root proxy -> session.recordStateDep('user'), returns proxy for `user` value with base 'user'. The func returns that proxy; we unwrap at end to raw. Good.\n\nBut how does a reducer selector's `runReducerSelector` work? Let's define:\n```ts\nrunReducerSelector(key, state, props) {\n  const meta = this.selectors.get(key)!\n  if (meta.inProgress) throw circular...\n  const session = new Session(this, meta)\n  push session\n  try {\n    const rootProxy = session.getRootProxy(state)\n    const result = rootProxy[key] // triggers recordStateDep(key) and returns proxy\n    const raw = this.unwrap(result)\n    const {dirty, cause} = this.checkDirty(meta)  // hmm need deps before check? Actually checkDirty uses meta.dependencies from previous run. On first run, deps empty -> dirty.\n    // But we already computed result. We need to know if we should return cached. If not dirty and hasCached, return cached before computing result. So check dirty first.\n    ...\n  }\n}\n```\nActually we should check dirty BEFORE computing result to avoid unnecessary work. But as discussed, for non-reducer selectors we need to evaluate args before checking dirty (to catch selector-output changes). For reducer selectors, they only depend on state leaves, and state versions are updated by middleware **before** evaluation. So we can check dirty first and skip rootProxy computation if not dirty. Good.\n\nSo `runReducerSelector`:\n1. circular check\n2. push session\n3. `const {dirty, cause} = this.checkDirty(meta)`\n4. if !dirty && hasCached: return cached\n5. else compute: rootProxy = session.getRootProxy(state); result = rootProxy[key]; raw = unwrap(result)\n6. commit deps, propagate, update meta, return raw.\n\nFor user selectors, we compute args first then check dirty. Let's also consider that for user selectors, `checkDirty` uses meta.dependencies (leaf deps) and versions. The args computation may trigger A's propagation which updates versions. Good.\n\nNow `propagate(meta, oldVal, newVal)`:\n- `const changes = diffPaths(oldVal, newVal, meta.name)` if oldVal !== newVal. Actually `diffPaths` handles equality. It returns changed leaf paths relative to meta.name, e.g. `user.name`, `user.age`, or `capitalizedName`.\n- For each changed path, `this.bumpPath(path)` which increments versions for path and all ancestors.\n- Note: `bumpPath` should also handle ancestor derivation: for `user.name`, bump `user.name` and `user`. For `data.map:a`, bump `data.map:a` and `data`.\n\nBut careful: `diffPaths(oldVal, newVal, meta.name)` for a selector that returns a primitive: if old=1,new=2, returns `[meta.name]` (e.g. `['bookId']`). Bump `bookId`. Good.\nFor a selector returning object with `name` changed: returns `['userInfo.name']`, bump `userInfo.name` and `userInfo`.\n\nNow `bumpPath`:\n```ts\nbumpPath(path) {\n  // path may contain '.map:' and '.set:' \u2014 ancestor splitting needs care\n  const parts = splitPath(path)\n  let cur = ''\n  for (const part of parts) {\n    cur = cur ? cur + '.' + part : part\n    this.versions.set(cur, (this.versions.get(cur) ?? 0) + 1)\n  }\n}\n```\n`splitPath('user.name')` => ['user','name']; `splitPath('data.map:a')` => ['data','map:a']; `splitPath('list.0')` => ['list','0']. Ancestors: 'data', 'data.map:a'. Good.\n\nNow `checkDirty(meta)`:\n```ts\ncheckDirty(meta) {\n  if (!meta.hasCached) return {dirty:true, cause:null}\n  for (const d of meta.dependencies) {\n    const current = this.versions.get(d) ?? 0\n    const last = meta.lastDepVersions.get(d)\n    if (last === undefined || current !== last) {\n      const kind = meta.depKinds.get(d)\n      const cause = kind === 'selector' ? `selector:${d}` : d\n      return {dirty:true, cause}\n    }\n  }\n  return {dirty:false, cause:null}\n}\n```\nBut note: on first run, meta.dependencies is empty, so checkDirty returns dirty=true due to !hasCached. Good. After first run, if deps empty (a selector with no dependencies), it will never be dirty \u2014 fine.\n\nWait, there's an issue: when meta's dependencies change between runs (e.g., `book` initially reads `books.1`, later reads `books.2`), `lastDepVersions` for `books.2` is undefined -> dirty. Good. But stale deps from previous run that are no longer used should be removed (we set meta.dependencies = new set each commit).\n\nNow `commitDeps(meta, session)`:\n```ts\nconst dependencies = new Set<string>()\nconst depKinds = new Map<string,'selector'|'state'>()\nfor (const path of session.stateDeps) {\n  dependencies.add(path); depKinds.set(path,'state')\n}\nfor (const entry of session.pendingDeps.values()) {\n  if (entry.paths.size === 0) {\n    dependencies.add(entry.selector); depKinds.set(entry.selector,'selector')\n  } else {\n    for (const p of entry.paths) {\n      dependencies.add(p); depKinds.set(p,'state')\n    }\n  }\n}\nmeta.dependencies = dependencies\nmeta.depKinds = depKinds\n// recompute dependents\nmeta.dependents.clear()  // wait dependents are on the *dependency* selectors, not this one.\n```\n\nDependents: For each selector S, `S.dependents` is the set of names that depend on S. We need to update dependents when any selector's dependencies change. Instead of storing dependents on each meta and updating on commit, we can compute dependents lazily in `selectorHealth()` by iterating all selectors' dependencies and building a reverse map. That's simpler and avoids stale updates. But the health API requires `dependents` per selector. We can compute on demand:\n```ts\nbuildDependents() {\n  const map = new Map<string, Set<string>>()\n  for (const meta of this.selectors.values()) {\n    for (const dep of meta.dependencies) {\n      const root = firstSegment(dep)\n      if (this.selectors.has(root)) {\n        if (!map.has(root)) map.set(root, new Set())\n        map.get(root)!.add(meta.name)\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return map\n}\n```\nBut dependencies like `user.name` where `user` is a root reducer selector: first segment `user` is a selector, so dependents correct. For dep `capitalizedName` (selector kind), first segment `capitalizedName` -> dependents correct. For a dep `someStatePath` that isn't a selector (rare), skip.\n\nHowever, the health `dependents` array should list \"local names of selectors that depend on this one\". So if B depends on `user.name`, then `user.dependents` includes B. Good.\n\nNow `topologicalOrder`: \"An array of selector names sorted by their evaluation order in the dependency graph.\" We can compute a topological sort of the selector dependency graph (selector-level). Use Kahn's algorithm: nodes = all selector names; edges from dependency selector to dependent (if first segment is a selector name). But dependencies that are leaf paths into a selector (`user.name`) create an edge from `user` to the dependent. Dependencies that are whole selector names (`capitalizedName`) also edge. Dependencies that are state leaves not matching any selector (e.g., root reducers are selectors so they match). If no cycles, produce order. But we should detect cycles at build time and throw, so at health time graph is acyclic. Topological order by evaluation order: a selector that is depended upon should come before its dependents. So edges `dep -> dependent`, topological sort produces deps first.\n\nBut there's a nuance: root reducer selectors depend on state (no selector deps), so they have no incoming edges from selectors; they'll be first. Derived selectors later. Good.\n\nNow, `selectorHealth()` should be available when atomicSelectors true. We'll define on logic:\n```ts\nlogic.selectorHealth = () => {\n  const engine = getAtomicEngine(logic)\n  const dependents = engine.buildDependents()\n  const selectors = {}\n  for (const [name, meta] of engine.selectors) {\n    selectors[name] = {\n      dependencies: [...meta.dependencies].sort(),\n      dependents: [...(dependents.get(name) ?? [])].sort(),\n      evaluations: meta.evaluations,\n      dirtyCause: meta.dirtyCause,\n    }\n  }\n  return { selectors, topologicalOrder: engine.topologicalOrder() }\n}\n```\n\nNow, the engine's `selectors` map includes reducer selectors (created via `createReducerSelector`) and user selectors. It should include **all** `logic.selectors`? In Kea, `logic.selectors` includes connected values too (from connect), which are not created by this engine. Should they appear in health? The task says selectors map with names. Connected values are also selectors on the logic. Ideally include them, but they don't have local metadata. For simplicity, we can include only locally-created selectors (reducers + user selectors). But health tests might expect all? The spec says \"selectors: { [name]: ... }\" \u2014 probably local ones. I'll include locally-created ones. Connected values can be omitted or included with empty deps. Let's think: if a test does `logic.selectors` with only user/reducer selectors, fine.\n\nNow, circular detection at build time. In `selectors()` builder, we'll do a static pass. Let's implement a function `detectCircularSelectorDependencies(selectorInputs, logic)`:\n- For each selector name, call `input` with a recording proxy `s` that records property accesses. But `input` is `selectorInputs[key][0]` (the first element of the tuple), and it expects `(s, p)`. We can call it with `(recordingSelectorsProxy, propSelectors)`. The recording proxy's get trap records the property name and returns a dummy function.\n- Build graph name -> Set<referenced names>.\n- Also include reducer selector deps? Reducer selectors don't depend on other selectors, so no edges.\n- Detect cycle via DFS. If cycle, throw `[KEA] Circular dependency detected`.\n- This static pass runs during build. But calling `input` a second time (once for static, once for real) could have side effects if user input function is impure. But Kea input functions are expected pure-ish. The current code calls `input` once to get args. We can avoid double call by capturing both the recorded deps and the args in one pass: the recording proxy can also return the real call functions? Actually the proxy getter for `logic.selectors` returns the real atomic call function; but to record deps we need to know which names were accessed. We can wrap the returned function so that when `input` runs, each get records the name. We can have the proxy getter both record the name and return the real call function. Then a single `input(...)` call gives us both args and static deps. That's clean.\n\nSo in atomic mode, when building selectors:\n1. `const staticDeps = new Map<string, Set<string>>()`\n2. `const selectorsProxy = new Proxy(logic.selectors, { get(target, prop) { record prop; return engine.callSelectorFunction(String(prop)) } })`\n   - But `logic.selectors` currently contains placeholder functions. We pass this proxy to `input`.\n3. `const args = input(selectorsProxy, propSelectors)`\n4. After all selectors' args are collected, detect cycles from staticDeps.\n5. Then create atomic selectors using args.\n\nBut there's an ordering issue: the first loop in current code pre-adds placeholders and collects keys; the second loop processes entries. We can integrate static deps into the second loop: when we call `input`, the proxy records deps. So:\n- In the first loop, we can't call input yet (need selectorInputs and keys). We can create placeholders first.\n- In the second loop, for each entry, call input with recording proxy, get args and static deps; then create selector.\n- After processing all entries (so all deps known), run cycle detection. But if a cycle exists, we'd have already created some selectors. That's okay; we can throw before/after. Better to detect after collecting all static deps but before creating atomic selectors? But we need args to create. We can collect all `entryInfos` first (name, args, staticDeps) in a first pass, detect cycles, then create selectors in second pass. That matches current code's two-pass structure. Let's do:\n  - Pass 1: for each name, check duplicate, call `input` with recording proxy to get args and record static deps. Store args. \n  - Detect cycles.\n  - Pass 2: for each name, create atomic selector with args and func, add to logic.\n\nBut calling `input` before creating placeholders? Current code's first loop adds placeholders because input functions may reference selectors defined later. If we call input in pass 1 before placeholders exist, the recording proxy getter can still return call functions (which don't require placeholders). So we might not need placeholders at all in atomic mode. Let's keep placeholders for safety? The recording proxy returns call functions that look up by name at call time, so order doesn't matter. So we can skip placeholders.\n\nLet me restructure the atomic path in `selectors()`:\n```ts\nif (getContext().options.atomicSelectors) {\n  atomicSelectorsBuilder(logic, selectorInputs)\n  return\n}\n```\nAnd `atomicSelectorsBuilder`:\n```ts\nconst engine = getAtomicEngine(logic)\nconst propSelectors = ... (same as current)\nconst entries: {name, args, func, memoizeOptions}[] = []\nconst staticDeps = new Map<string, Set<string>>()\nconst recordingProxy = new Proxy(logic.selectors, {\n  get(target, prop) {\n    const name = String(prop)\n    // record static dep for current selector being processed\n    if (currentName) {\n      if (!staticDeps.has(currentName)) staticDeps.set(currentName, new Set())\n      staticDeps.get(currentName)!.add(name)\n    }\n    return engine.callSelectorFunction(name)\n  }\n})\nlet currentName = null\nfor (const [name, arr] of Object.entries(selectorInputs)) {\n  if (typeof logic.selectors[name] !== 'undefined') throw...\n  if (!arr) throw...\n  const [input, func, memoizeOptions] = arr\n  currentName = name\n  const args = input(recordingProxy, propSelectors) // this records static deps\n  currentName = null\n  // validate args...\n  entries.push({name, args, func})\n}\n// detect cycles in staticDeps\ndetectCircular(staticDeps, logic)\n// create selectors\nfor (const {name, args, func} of entries) {\n  const selector = engine.createSelector(name, args, func)\n  addSelectorAndValue(logic, name, selector)\n}\n```\n\nBut wait: the proxy getter records only direct property accesses in the `input` function. It does not record accesses in the `func` (compute) \u2014 those are dynami